# ec/tools/verify_gap_text.py --check, wired into the cheap gate. Prepared here
# rather than landed, for the reason docs/ci/agent-gates-capture-claims.patch
# gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` is copied from the agent-pipeline
# template and the pipeline's push token has no `workflow` scope, so a branch
# editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than at the start of it.
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this one across a re-copy too, as its item 4,
# which already carries the arm this patch applies.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-gap-text-check.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Added 2026-09-25 (issue #745). `docs/agent-pipeline.md` item 4 had the recipe
# and the justification for this since issue #151, and no patch file: a human
# landing "the prepared gate wiring" had three files, one of which existed only
# as prose. The arm is the one item 4 states verbatim.
#
# Why the cheap tier and not agent-gates-deep.sh: what `--check` reads is the
# committed firmware and the committed `.asm` listings, and the standard
# library's `csv`. No assembler, no Ghidra, no network -- so by the split
# `docs/agent-pipeline.md` records, it belongs beside `check_register_counts`,
# not in the deep tier. It measures 0.30 s here, against a cheap tier recorded
# at 5.9 s, on 2026-09-25. That is one runner's figure and the ratio is the
# point; item 6 gives the same caveat for `call_graph.py`.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not: these are the 143 instructions
# `sdas8051` cannot express, so no assembler reaches them and before this tool
# no check read them at all. `--check` recomputes the set from the committed
# listings rather than transcribing it, and every declined instruction records
# which predicate declined it, so a sixth form with no cross-decode handler
# fails the run instead of passing as coverage. The evidence is comparative
# rather than constructive -- two decoders, no code in common, reading the same
# byte column -- and the tool's own header says so at length. **Until a human
# lands this, no commit runs it**, and the verdicts in
# `ec/ghidra/gap-text-check.csv` can go stale in an otherwise-green commit.
# It reads bytes and listings and nothing else: no capture is opened, no EC is
# opened, no register is read back.
#
# One thing to know before landing it. `--check` was red on the tree this was
# cut from, on one row's `row_name` column: a function rename had landed and
# `ec/ghidra/gap-text-check.csv` had not been regenerated since. That is fixed
# in the same branch, by running the tool's own `--report` (one row, one
# column, no assembler), so `--check` is green as prepared. An earlier cut of
# this patch, made before that was noticed, would have turned the cheap gate red
# on landing -- the cheapest way to make a gate get switched off, and the reason
# `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh` carries a long comment about the census tool
# being deliberately left out of the gate while it is red for the same reason.
#
# The comment it adds at the call site says the same thing where the next reader
# of the gate will see it, rather than only here.

diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
index 125d647..4e7194a 100755
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -119,6 +119,7 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
   for tool in ec/tools/gen_xdata_symbols.py \
               ec/tools/build_ec_decompile.py \
               ec/tools/verify_reassembly.py \
+              ec/tools/verify_gap_text.py \
               ec/tools/merge_annotation_shards.py \
               ec/tools/grade_name_basis.py \
               ec/tools/group_functions.py \
@@ -162,6 +163,20 @@ check_ghidra_tooling() {
         python3 "$tool" --verify-provenance \
           --base 08b72e2 --migration a56b3bb --listings-from 8c7985e || rc=1
         ;;
+      # The 143 instructions sdas8051 cannot express, cross-decoded by
+      # disasm8051.py, which shares no code with Ghidra's SLEIGH. What the
+      # re-encode above cannot reach was read by no check at all before
+      # verify_gap_text.py existed, so the committed verdicts in
+      # ec/ghidra/gap-text-check.csv could go stale in an otherwise-green
+      # commit (docs/findings.md §11a). --check is the whole of it: the set is
+      # recomputed from the committed listings rather than transcribed, and an
+      # instruction declined for a reason with no cross-decode handler fails
+      # the run rather than being skipped, so a sixth form cannot pass as
+      # coverage. Cheap tier for the reason the comment above gives: python3
+      # and the committed firmware, no assembler, no Ghidra, no network.
+      *verify_gap_text.py)
+        python3 "$tool" --check || rc=1
+        ;;
       # The annotation layer's grading and grouping. The four cross-field
       # rules do reach this gate already, indirectly, because
       # build_ec_decompile.py and bios_extract.py import row_problems() --
